A||N||3||Vlad||Grand Am's  best year for breaks?||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||11-25-2000||08:55 PM||||Hello<P>So going to perform a rear breaks swap, I'm wondering what is the best year for upgrade.<BR>88 or 89?<P>Also, for some reason I can't find a link to<BR>the page where the swap prosedure was described for front breaks as well.<BR>Can somebody send me a link....?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||11-26-2000||12:09 AM||||So a search for Beretta brakes. That's what I did. There is tons of information about in on the net.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-26-2000||08:15 AM||||There were 2 different styles.  '88 and '89 should be identical.  I think it was after '90 or '91 when they switched to larger rotors.<P>Try this link:  <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||11-26-2000||08:55 AM||||The Grand-Am used 9 45/64" rotors until '89. In '90 the 10 13/64" rotors were optional as a special performance package. In '91 the 10 13/64" brakes became standard. The Berretta uses the same small rotors until 91, and the larger rotors from 92 and up.<P>So to answer your question, there is no difference between the 88 and 89 rotors. The larger rotors are only 1/2" bigger overall, I'm not sure that would make a noticable difference.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
